Since {AB} and {BC} are medial, the squares on {AB} and {BC} are also medial. But twice the rectangle {AB} by {BC} is rational, therefore the sum of the squares on {AB} and {BC} is incommensurable with twice the rectangle {AB} by {BC}.

<div class="just">cf. <a href="/elements/books/bookII/propositions/propII7/">II.7</a><br><a href="/elements/books/bookX/propositions/propX16/">X.16</a></div>

Therefore twice the rectangle {AB} by {BC} is also incommensurable with the remainder, the square on {AC}, since, if the whole is incommensurable with one of the magnitudes, then the original magnitudes are also incommensurable.

But twice the rectangle {AB} by {BC} is rational, therefore the square on {AC} is irrational, therefore {AC} is irrational. Let it be called a first apotome of a medial straight line. [!just X.Def.I.4]

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
----
guide: This proposition is used for a few later propositions in Book X starting with @X.80.
